[32m+[m[32m# Viewport travel[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mA scrolled viewport walks to its new offset instead of cutting to it. The plan[m
[32m+[m[32mbelow is what the recording scrolls: every step is numbered so a frame taken[m
[32m+[m[32mmid-travel names the rows it is passing.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m## Why[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 01 — A page down replaced the screen between two frames.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 02 — Nothing on screen said which direction the content went.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 03 — The scrollbar thumb was the only clue, and it arrived first.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 04 — A reader had to re-find their place after every notch.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 05 — The rest of the surface already travels: selections, bands, gauges.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m## The value[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 06 — One number, `SettleValue`, on the shared motion clock.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 07 — The curve is `MOTION.move`: spring 260/30/1, the selection curve.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 08 — The first offset a viewport is given lands. Nothing animates a mount.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 09 — Every later offset travels.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 10 — A target changed mid-flight keeps the velocity it already had.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 11 — A wheel notch held down is therefore one continuous travel.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m## One value, two readings[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 12 — `getScrollOffset()` answers with the TARGET.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 13 — The keyboard lives at the target: page down twice pages twice.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 14 — `render()` slices the rows at the TRAVELLED value, rounded.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 15 — The thumb reads the same travelled value.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 16 — So the bar can never report a scroll the rows have not made.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 17 — A thumb that leads its rows is the bug this ordering forbids.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m## What travels and what lands[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 18 — A wheel notch travels.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 19 — An arrow travels.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 20 — A page travels.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 21 — A jump to a section of the table of contents travels.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 22 — Both ends land: home, end, and the tail of a live transcript.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 23 — A viewport following its own tail must never lag behind the tail.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 24 — A re-layout lands: the content changed under the offset.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 25 — A change smaller than two rows lands: there is nothing to watch.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 26 — A jump further than two screens lands: a blur is not a reading.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 27 — A pre-windowed viewport lands: the caller slices its own rows.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m## The hosts[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 28 — The fullscreen agent transcript viewer.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 29 — The plan review overlay, which is the surface this file is shown in.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 30 — Both are the viewports that outlive a single render.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 31 — Each lends the viewport its own repaint and takes it back on dispose.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 32 — `display.transitions: off` paints byte for byte what it painted before.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m## The suite[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 33 — Travel and monotonicity: the offset never goes backwards en route.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 34 — Frame count pinned against a reference `MOTION.move` animation.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 35 — The thumb never leads the rows, sampled every frame of a travel.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 36 — Single row, both ends, and a jump too far all land in one frame.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 37 — A travel survives the renders that happen during it.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 38 — Content shrinking under a live travel clamps the painted value.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 39 — A mode switch mid-travel lands rather than paints a stale row.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 40 — Motion off is byte identity, and dispose returns the clock.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m## The gate[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 41 — Fourteen mutations, fourteen reds.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 42 — The rows paint the target again.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 43 — The thumb reads the target.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 44 — A render lands the travel it finds.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 45 — No jump is ever too far.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 46 — A pre-windowed viewport travels a value nobody reads.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 47 — The travel floor is gone.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 48 — The painted value is not clamped.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 49 — The offset paints unrounded.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 50 — The viewport travels on the gauge's curve.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 51 — The curve option is ignored.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 52 — Every one of them red, and the restore proven by checksum.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m## The proof[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 53 — Two arms of the same scene, recorded in a real terminal.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 54 — Before: the offset cuts, and consecutive frames share no rows.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 55 — After: the offset walks, and the frames between hold the rows between.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 56 — The strips below are eight consecutive frames of one page down.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 57 — Read them left to right and the rows slide.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 58 — Read the before strip and there are two states with nothing between.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m## Notes[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 59 — `MOTION.move` was in the curve table before anything used it.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 60 — `SettleValue` gained a curve option rather than a second spring owner.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 61 — One owner for a number that walks, two curves for two speeds.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 62 — The gauge keeps `MOTION.settle`, which is slower on purpose.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 63 — A scroll is a reading, not a report, so it is the faster of the two.[m
[32m+[m[32mStep 64 — End of plan.[m
